THE LATE SHOW by Michael Connelly

2017. Allen and Unwin. Paperback.

I'd read the first 70 pages of this book and enjoyed the new Renee
Ballard character and then put it aside for some reason. I've since
returned to it and given it a fresh full read and found it very
satisfying.

Sometimes in a Michael Connelly thriller you get two crimes in a book,
this time there's a three in one as in a single night Renee Ballard
gets a stolen credit card fraud case, a shootout in a nightclub as a
criminal tries to set up a policeman so he can plea-bargain his way to
a lesser sentence, and a transgender prostitute being punched to
near-death by someone with brass knuckles.

The challenges Ballard endures in her life as a female police
detective (harassment, come-ons, fights with criminals twice her size,
meaningless unfulfilling sex) are well described in this
book. Ballard's closest relationships are with her dog and her
grandmother.

The nightclub shooting sees a criminal wearing a wire try to
incriminate a cop. The wire malfunctions, alerting the cop, and three
people are shot in a booth and two waiters are collateral damage.

Ballard's ex-partner is also murdered by the cop, but leaves enough
clever clues that Ballard is able to find the culprit. A very nice
touch in the book is Ballard setting up a journalist and capturing an
illegal meeting between the journalist and a high-up police chief.

One technique I hate in a lot of crime books is "You've loved this
book so much that here's the first 20 pages of the next thriller by
the same author". For once in my life I'm actually reading the teaser
back pages of the upcoming (November 2017) Bosch thriller, Two Kinds
of Truth. I could very well make that my next book.

